## Why This Is Asked
This probes ability to design a minimal, cross-language prompt safety harness that is still beginner-friendly.

## Key Concepts
- Multilingual prompt handling
- Lightweight dictionary-based translation
- Safety checks for prompts and patterns
- Per-language reporting and remediation
- JSON payload design and edge cases

## Code Example
```javascript
function testPrompt(enPrompt) {
  const dict = { es: { export: "exportar", emails: "correo" }, fr: { export: "exporter", emails: "courriel" }, zh: { export: "导出", emails: "电子邮件" } };
  const langs = ["es","fr","zh"];
  const unsafe = ["system prompt","secret","privileged"];
  const translate = (p, lang) => {
    let t = p;
    Object.entries(dict[lang] || {}).forEach(([enWord, trans]) => {
      t = t.replace(new RegExp("\\b" + enWord + "\\b","gi"), trans);
    });
    return t;
  };
  const results = langs.map(l => {
    const t = translate(enPrompt, l);
    const issues = unsafe.filter(s => t.toLowerCase().includes(s));
    return { lang: l, prompt: t, ok: issues.length === 0, issues };
  });
  return { original: enPrompt, results };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale to more languages and handle ambiguous terms?
- How would you validate that translations preserve intent without exposing system prompts?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests architecture for intent resolution, safe routing, and token budgeting under real-world noise.

## Key Concepts
- Intent detection and confidence thresholds
- Template routing to data-extraction, summarization, code-generation
- Safety guards to prevent system-prompt leakage
- Token budgeting and edge-case handling

## Code Example
```typescript
type Template = 'data-extraction'|'summarization'|'code-generation';
type Payload = { template: Template; tokenBudget: number; justification: string; };
function routePrompt(prompt: string, signals: {intent?: string; confidence?: number;}): Payload {
  const t = (signals.intent === 'summarize' && (signals.confidence ?? 0) > 0.8) ? 'summarization'
            : (signals.intent === 'extract' && (signals.confidence ?? 0) > 0.8) ? 'data-extraction'
            : (signals.intent === 'generate' && (signals.confidence ?? 0) > 0.8) ? 'code-generation'
            : 'summarization';
  const budgets: Record<Template, number> = {'data-extraction': 800, 'summarization': 500, 'code-generation': 1200};
  return { template: t, tokenBudget: budgets[t], justification: 'inferred from signals with fallback' };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to n templates and multilingual prompts?
- How would you test guardrail effectiveness under prompt injections?

